INT. ROOFTOP GREENHOUSE - MORNING

Glass walls, fogged. Tomato vines climbing strings. A cracked radio on the potting bench. The city sprawls beyond, gray and sleeping.

A WOMAN (30s, gardening gloves, hair tied up with twine) shuffles in with a mug and a stack of unopened mail. She sets the mug down. Taps a vine. Checks a leaf.

She sorts the envelopes. Bills. A catalog. A pale blue one, handwritten.

She stops.

She turns it over. Her name is on the front. She reads it aloud.

WOMAN
Imogen.

She says it again, softer, like testing a stair.

IMOGEN
Imogen Hartley-Voss.

She sits on an overturned bucket. The envelope trembles slightly in her fingers.

IMOGEN (CONT'D)
(to the plants)
Don't look at me.

She tears the flap. A single sheet. She unfolds it and lays it flat on her knee.

IMOGEN (V.O.)
(reading)
Dear Imogen. I know you will not answer this. I would not.

She stops reading aloud. Her lips keep moving. The radio crackles.

Behind her, a tomato drops from the vine and rolls across the floor, comes to a stop against her boot.

IMOGEN (V.O.)
(reading, continued)
I have been thinking about the greenhouse, and how you said plants grow towards whatever is already warm.

Imogen folds the letter in half. In half again. She holds it against her chest.

She picks up the tomato. She wipes it on her shirt. She bites into it, juice running down her wrist.

IMOGEN
(mouth full)
Still warm.

She laughs, once, quietly. Then she gets up, and finds a pen on the bench, and turns the envelope over to write.
